  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">South Asia Group for Energy (SAGE) 2.0</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">(USAID South Asia Regional):  .This is a partnership between USAID and a consortium with the U.S. Department of Energy (DOE) and three national laboratories: Lawrence Berkeley National Laboratory, National Renewable Energy Laboratory, and Pacific Northwest National Laboratory. It will support the Indo-Pacific Strategy and the U.S-India Climate and Clean Energy Agenda 2030 Partnership by undertaking analysis and modeling on energy sector opportunities throughout South Asia, equipping USAID and partner governments with information on clean energy transition opportunities and providing support for sustainable and secure energy markets across the Indo-Pacific. It will be a key mechanism to deliver on the Indo-Pacific Strategy as well on the Sustainable Growth Pillar under the Strategic Clean Energy Partnership, one of the two tracks under the U.S.-India Climate and Clean Energy Agenda 2030 Partnership. It facilitates access to technical expertise within DOE labs and other relevant USG and private sector partners for knowledge sharing to support Pillar 1 (Climate Actions) and Pillar 3 (Adaptation and Resilience) under the Climate Action and Finance Mobilization Dialogue.. .This mechanism will undertake to model the impact of changing economic and environmental conditions on the reliability of South Asia&apos;s power sector and assess aspects related to grid management. The consortium undertakes analyses to support the promotion of clean energy technologies, strengthen grid security and reliability for the clean energy transition, and assess the impacts of climate change on hydro-resources. It partners with institutions to strengthen their modeling and analytical capacity for clean energy. It will also leverage the U.S. partnership with India through collaborations with Indian technical institutions, including the India Energy Modeling Forum established with NITI Aayog (India&apos;s premier think tank), and explore higher education partnerships to promote clean energy and climate a</narrative>
  </description>
